title: Writing the format's own lines: title and needs
created: 2026-09-18T17:55:05Z
updated: 2026-09-18T17:55:05Z
effort: M
area: cli
handoff:

pidly set writes project fields and refuses the format's:

  pidly set 1 title "a new title"   -> pidly: no field 'title' in the schema
  pidly set 3 needs ""              -> pidly: no field 'needs' in the schema

Which is correct as a rule — the schema declares project fields and lines 1,
2 and 3 belong to the format — but it leaves two ordinary operations with no
command at all:

  retitle                   sed -i '3s/.*/title: .../'
  add or drop a dependency  sed -i '2s/.*/needs: 24/'

Both are real ongoing work rather than corrections at creation time. new -n
wires dependencies only when the ticket is made, and a ticket that turns out
to be blocked later is the normal case, not the exception.

The danger is specific: a hand sed on line 2 or 3 has nothing checking what
it writes. A needs: naming a number that does not exist is a check finding
the author will not see until CI, and a botched line 3 stops being a title
line at all. A command validates before writing; sed does not.

Decide the surface: whether set learns the format's lines as a special case,
whether a separate command owns them, or whether needs gets its own verb
because adding and dropping one dependency is not the same operation as
replacing the line. Adding one dependency to a ticket that has three should
not require the caller to retype the other two.

Done when retitling and changing dependencies need no sed, a dependency on a
number that does not exist is refused at write time rather than found by
check, and the reference still documents the hand idiom beside the command.
